fore he  ordered his forces to retire  although on this occasion I remember  hearing delivered by our colonel in  Indian the finest War speech I ever  heard  when you first saw the light it was said  of you a man-child is born you must  prove today whether or not this saying  of you was true the Sun that hangs over  our heads has no death no end of days it  will continue indefinitely to rise and  to set but with you it is different man  must die sometime and since he must die  he can find no nobler death than that  which overtakes him while fighting for  his home his fires and his country  [Music]  The Retreat left the family's resident  in the country without any protection  and as the enemy was destroying  everything in the way of their March it  was important that each family and  non-combatant decide and quickly whether  to remain at home and Trust the mercy of  the invading enemy or load up such few  things as they were compelled to have  into wagons and as our forces were doing  following Retreat towards the Choctaw  Nation  in general our soldiers were poorly clad  and most of the time my company  presented a Motley appearance the  Confederacy being hard run had very  little in the way of clothing to issue  to the man on the part of the country  and we were never very presentable so  when we called a prisoner from the other  side we generally stripped him clean of  his wearing apparel as we desired they  being always better than our own and  placed upon him instead such as our own  old Duds as he could wear how government  had issued our men certain wool hats  which appeared to be manufactured of the  plain sheep's wool without any coloring  while the hatter seemed not to have  seriously concerned themselves about the  Symmetry and Poise of any of any  individual hat  one such hat that it well nice served  its time and collected its full share of  dust and discolorations was owned and  worn by machanelli being a young man of  decided the Indian taste in matters of  dress and orientation he embellished it  in true Aboriginal Style with one of the  plumes of the wing of the falcon  commonly known in the country as the  chicken hawk the shaft of which had been  carefully paired away and rendered so  thin pliant and limber that the least  air disturbance gave it a vibratory or  rather tremulous motion highly  responsive to the Indian's idea of  Aesthetics  this feather he'd securely fastened in  the Apex of his hat where it had for  considerable time done service doubtless  to the admiration of his comrades in war  [Music]  as we were riding along discussing the  situation we reached a small Choctaw  Farm of probably not over 10 acres an  extent on our right planted in corn as  we rode silently along I observed ahead  of us horses which appeared to be tied  to the fence we saw at first no Riders  although the horses were saddled and  bridled we halted and listened for the  sound of voices but heard none we then  stealthily crept nearer where we saw I  think two men lying near up to the fence  fast asleep my blonde friend seemed to  think that this was our time to get in  our work on the enemy and suggested that  we rush upon them and dispatch them  while they slept he'd already impressed  me as being not very practical in some  of his ideas and Views and fearing he  might be dependent on the certain  operation of his little old pistol which  might fail to work when we reached the  dispatching moment and cause us to be  killed or taken prisoners I asked to  examine the gun at a glance I became  satisfied that there was great danger it  would not fire at all and a y eyes view  of the situation manifestly dictated  that we go slow in the proposed work of  death but we had no clubs at hand with  which we might Club them to death and I  was not sure that my blonde friend and I  were able to pounce upon these men and  choke them to death I counseled that we  leave them to the continued enjoyment of  their Slumbers while we turned out into  the woods continuing our course but not  traveling the roads  sometime after this a raid was planned  to cross the enemy's line and attack a  party of Haymakers who were encamped on  the Prairie north of the verdugri river  under protection of a Detachment of  soldiers from Fort Gibson it seemed to  be the policy of the military  authorities to use our Indian Brigade  not so much for service outside of the  Indian Territory as a menace to the  enemy's Southern Line we were constantly  watching for weak places or  insufficiently protected interests where  we might run in deliver a damaging blow  and fly quickly to the cover of our base  which in the later years of the war was  in the Choctaw Nation it was a kind of  warfare that was suited to the Indian  character and in carrying out the policy  we continually attracted the notice of  the enemy to ourselves thus keeping him  from concentrating with others of his  forces and aiding in giving effective  battle to some of our larger armies  which had already all the enemy that  they would care to deal with but now it  appeared that the choctaws none of whom  were with us in this raid had the  reputation with the enemy of being  heartless and of never sparing the life  of any enemy that came into their power  this was untrue but nevertheless  confidently believed by the enemy when  some of our soldiers made the Hideous  yells and charged the enemies left they  were mistaken for the deadly Choctaw for  as the enemy thought who but he could  utter such blood curdling noises they  reasoned that to yield their position  and trust a flight for safety would mean  their certain capture and Massacre on  the Prairies adjoining their camps by  these notorious and bloodthirsty  choctaws the only alternative being that  of remaining where they were and fight  it out the latter alternative they  adopted and those who were giving way to  flight returned and put up such a stiff  resistance that we were after a while  forced to reconsider our plans and give  up the fight  in this case the entire number of men  volunteering to engage in the raid did  not I think exceed 300. major Howland of  one of the Cherokee regiments was placed  in command and in this number of Raiders  there were Cherokee and Creeks from both  of the creek regiments I was of the  number with a few men from my immediate  company I remember that my cousin  Valentine n mcanally elsewhere mentioned  as being a month older than myself who  was a member of my company was with me  on this occasion  The Raid so far was regarded by the men  as evidently successful although the  cost of one life and a painful wound  the man had captured and appropriated  many of the various articles of value  from the camps of the enemy when they  charged them now as they were on their  return they crossed the river and being  tired and anxious to examine and see  more closely the booty they had each  taken they halted on the south banks of  the river where the Bluffs were high and  still  the boys were much pleased many of them  with their halls and were busily engaged  in showing each other their goods and  trading swapping and buying them from  one another in high Glee going up out of  the Bluffs to the top I dismounted and  with my Bridal reins in my hands  followed my horse around while he was  picking the grass which was very good  here and awaited orders to March  before such order was given however and  while the trade was brisk upon and below  the Bluffs the enemy whom we had  ransacked and dispersed having rallied  got together in considerable Force crept  up through the bottoms on the opposite  side and Delta sir volley from the thick  brush the stream only being about 50  yards wide here which created a panic in  our trade and dire consternation among  the Traders men who had dropped their  Bridal Reigns giving their horses full  Liberty to graze on the grass at will  were now running to and fro calling to  others to catch their horses for them  the horses when approached by frightened  men would in turn become frightened at  the unusual actions of their masters and  Scamper away which but for its  seriousness would have been in the  highest degree laughable we then  discovered that our commanding officer  major Howland was not with us there were  those who declared that the officer was  wounded by the enemy's first volley  Captain Sam Gunter of the Cherokees  would probably be next in command and  indeed was already rallying the men and  apparently waiting to get them into some  sort of shape for resistance or attack  of the enemy what to do was the question  that was uppermost to us  we held a council of War as it may be  called on Horseback when Captain Gunter  declared that if we could get a  sufficient number of men to follow him  he would attack the enemy our men  however exhibited no mind for engaging  in his proposed Enterprise I told the  captain for one I would follow him being  Burgess said that he would also go and  while there may have been others who  profit their services I do not recall  any  while we were talking Captain Gunter  decided that he would not make the  Venture as he said he himself was  wounded and in no proper condition for  leading a fight the captain however  could hardly have been called wounded as  what he designated as a wound as I  narrow call was only this one of the  enemy's bullets had struck the large  brass Army Buckle or clasp that fastened  his leather belt in front and without  going through it had reddened and  bruised his skin and probably Jarred him  considerably at the moment of impact  this was all  with this decision we as I must say  ignominiously left the field and our  commander without any effort toward  determining his fate or rendering him  any assistance a Detachment was sent out  in two days afterwards who found him  making his way back to camps over the  route traveling by his command and  brought him safely to camp  a short time after this I think it was a  new Commander came into our wing of the  theater of war and did some very  creditable work this was a general  Richard M Gano who before long in this  department received information of a  valuable train of wagons belonging to  the enemy and all loaded with supplies  for the post of Fort Gibson which was  moving down from Fort Scott Kansas he  determined to go after and meet these  people and if possible take them in  after several days of preparation some 2  000 men as I believe consisting of  creeks Seminoles Cherokees and Texans  with a battery of four pieces of  artillery were gotten in Readiness for  the Expedition I joined this Expedition  it having become my rule and purpose to  take part in all proposed raids against  the enemy it is perhaps right to explain  here that such inclinations did not  necessarily signify bravery courage or  desire in me for recklessly quoting and  facing danger as I found some of my  associates interpreted it but rather the  dominating influence of the somewhat  commonplace thirst for excitement and  desire for Action characteristic more or  less of all young people  from here we now moved on after bigger  game in two days thereafter as I now  recall we reach the encampment of the  enemy early in the morning the fires of  which we could clearly see for quite a  considerable distance before we were  sufficiently near to offer them battle  arriving however in Easy reach and it  being yet dark we formed an order of  battle and stood awaiting the light of  day when we could see how to meet and  dispute the men of which we had come so  far to see  while we were quietly awaiting the light  it appeared quite clear from the  boisterous and careless Talk of the  enemy which we could plainly hear that  there had been some drinking and that at  least some of the men were now under the  influence of drink  some of them called at us what are you  doing here what do you want some of our  men answered we are after you and your  mule teams and wagons presently one of  the enemy who evidently was drunk after  heaping upon a small manner of vile  epithets and curses started over our way  never ceasing his curses as he came he  came as I recall in such a way as to  reach the south end of our line as we  stood and presently one of the men  called to him halt  he appeared to pay no heed to the  challenge but continued his Advance when  we heard again the call to Halt this did  not check his movements so the third and  last challenge was made and within a few  moments a shot then another  and all was quiet  it was now light enough for us to see  what we should do and the order coming  to us to fire our men obeyed with  alacrity in spirit and things certainly  now began to appear decidedly war-like  before resuming and while waiting in  line for further orders some members of  my company having great faith in certain  Indian War medicine said to afford  protection against the casualties of  battle reduce the medicine Talisman or  whatever the article was I never saw it  and distributed it to those who decide  its protection which they proceeded to  use by rubbing it on and about the  clothing of the body and limbs as  thoroughly as they could within the  limited time they had for taking the  treatment  my first lieutenant superfe fixico  Thomas Benton as he was called in  English I distinctly recall offered to  supply me with a treatment saying aren't  you going to use this I declined his  Prophet kindness It's still being in me  to prove to these men that I was willing  to risk myself in the presence of danger  under the impulses of my innate courage  without resorting to the supposed  protection afforded by medicines or  talismans and proved to them that I  could meet the risks as Cooley as the  bravest soldier under me and was not the  man to shirk danger as some at one time  seemed to think I was  I'd loudly encouraged and cheered my men  from the first speaking always in Indian  until my voice gave way unto the  continued strain and was now only a  whisper and could scarcely be heard at  all Pleasant Porter always told me  afterwards that being with the charge  when the stockades were taken he looked  about him and saw not a single Creek  besides himself when there had been so  many on the firing line in the battle  proper he was just experiencing a  feeling of regret that the Creeks did  not have greater representation in this  crowning Act of actual Victory when  looking over a few steps from where he  stood he saw me the only other Creek and  felt an experience of relief it was a  sort of feeling as if the honor of the  creeks and this engagement in which we  were victorious had been sufficiently  Vindicated  one evening when the men had been some  hours reeling off to each other Yarns  funny and otherwise and conversation was  lagging I recall distinctly how Jackson  moon our way Rose from where he'd been  sitting starting to go to the fire to  light his pipe he remarked when under  our former Captain we made loud  complaints of his lack of courage to  lead us to battle we charged he was  cowardly I'm sure we have nothing to  complain of now  the former Captain was an old man and  really not a man for action and least of  all military action but he was put in  the office because he was old and was  regarded as one of the wise men of the  Eufaula town of Indians much had been  said in complaint against his inactivity  and this remark was meant for a  compliment to me and was so understood  by all  I never for a moment expected such a  compliment as Indians rarely ever say  things complementary to a person in his  presence this was therefore all the more  gratifying to me as proving that I'd  fully succeeded in satisfying my friends  and my enemies if I had any that I could  and would willingly go was far toward  meeting a dangerous proposition as any  other of my age and experience  I however have sometimes doubted whether  I was entitled to any credit for what  others regarded as courage in me for the  reasons that at the time I was but a boy  I probably did not fully appreciate the  dangers around me I don't think I did  the Brave and courageous man in my  present more mature judgment is he who  in his normal mental condition clearly  sees as he believes imminent danger and  even probable death before him and yet  without shrinking coldly faces and goes  into it  of course as he believes it is his duty  to do so  [Music]  foreign  [Music]

