er this I think it was a  new Commander came into our wing of the  theater of war and did some very  creditable work this was a general  Richard M Gano who before long in this  department received information of a  valuable train of wagons belonging to  the enemy and all loaded with supplies  for the post of Fort Gibson which was  moving down from Fort Scott Kansas he  determined to go after and meet these  people and if possible take them in  after several days of preparation some 2  000 men as I believe consisting of  creeks Seminoles Cherokees and Texans  with a battery of four pieces of  artillery were gotten in Readiness for  the Expedition I joined this Expedition  it having become my rule and purpose to  take part in all proposed raids against  the enemy it is perhaps right to explain  here that such inclinations did not  necessarily signify bravery courage or  desire in me for recklessly quoting and  facing danger as I found some of my  associates interpreted it but rather the  dominating influence of the somewhat  commonplace thirst for excitement and  desire for Action characteristic more or  less of all young people  from here we now moved on after bigger  game in two days thereafter as I now  recall we reach the encampment of the  enemy early in the morning the fires of  which we could clearly see for quite a  considerable distance before we were  sufficiently near to offer them battle  arriving however in Easy reach and it  being yet dark we formed an order of  battle and stood awaiting the light of  day when we could see how to meet and  dispute the men of which we had come so  far to see  while we were quietly awaiting the light  it appeared quite clear from the  boisterous and careless Talk of the  enemy which we could plainly hear that  there had been some drinking and that at  least some of the men were now under the  influence of drink  some of them called at us what are you  doing here what do you want some of our  men answered we are after you and your  mule teams and wagons presently one of  the enemy who evidently was drunk after  heaping upon a small manner of vile  epithets and curses started over our way  never ceasing his curses as he came he  came as I recall in such a way as to  reach the south end of our line as we  stood and presently one of the men  called to him halt  he appeared to pay no heed to the  challenge but continued his Advance when  we heard again the call to Halt this did  not check his movements so the third and  last challenge was made and within a few  moments a shot then another  and all was quiet  it was now light enough for us to see  what we should do and the order coming  to us to fire our men obeyed with  alacrity in spirit and things certainly  now began to appear decidedly war-like  before resuming and while waiting in  line for further orders some members of  my company having great faith in certain  Indian War medicine said to afford  protection against the casualties of  battle reduce the medicine Talisman or  whatever the article was I never saw it  and distributed it to those who decide  its protection which they proceeded to  use by rubbing it on and about the  clothing of the body and limbs as  thoroughly as they could within the  limited time they had for taking the  treatment  my first lieutenant superfe fixico  Thomas Benton as he was called in  English I distinctly recall offered to  supply me with a treatment saying aren't  you going to use this I declined his  Prophet kindness It's still being in me  to prove to these men that I was willing  to risk myself in the presence of danger  under the impulses of my innate courage  without resorting to the supposed  protection afforded by medicines or  talismans and proved to them that I  could meet the risks as Cooley as the  bravest soldier under me and was not the  man to shirk danger as some at one time  seemed to think I was  I'd loudly encouraged and cheered my men  from the first speaking always in Indian  until my voice gave way unto the  continued strain and was now only a  whisper and could scarcely be heard at  all Pleasant Porter always told me  afterwards that being with the charge  when the stockades were taken he looked  about him and saw not a single Creek  besides himself when there had been so  many on the firing line in the battle  proper he was just experiencing a  feeling of regret that the Creeks did  not have greater representation in this  crowning Act of actual Victory when  looking over a few steps from where he  stood he saw me the only other Creek and  felt an experience of relief it was a  sort of feeling as if the honor of the  creeks and this engagement in which we  were victorious had been sufficiently  Vindicated  one evening when the men had been some  hours reeling off to each other Yarns  funny and otherwise and conversation was  lagging I recall distinctly how Jackson  moon our way Rose from where he'd been  sitting starting to go to the fire to  light his pipe he remarked when under  our former Captain we made loud  complaints of his lack of courage to  lead us to battle we charged he was  cowardly I'm sure we have nothing to  complain of now  the former Captain was an old man and  really not a man for action and least of  all military action but he was put in  the office because he was old and was  regarded as one of the wise men of the  Eufaula town of Indians much had been  said in complaint against his inactivity  and this remark was meant for a  compliment to me and was so understood  by all  I never for a moment expected such a  compliment as Indians rarely ever say  things complementary to a person in his  presence this was therefore all the more  gratifying to me as proving that I'd  fully succeeded in satisfying my friends  and my enemies if I had any that I could  and would willingly go was far toward  meeting a dangerous proposition as any  other of my age and experience  I however have sometimes doubted whether  I was entitled to any credit for what  others regarded as courage in me for the  reasons that at the time I was but a boy  I probably did not fully appreciate the  dangers around me I don't think I did  the Brave and courageous man in my  present more mature judgment is he who  in his normal mental condition clearly  sees as he believes imminent danger and  even probable death before him and yet  without shrinking coldly faces and goes  into it  of course as he believes it is his duty  to do so  [Music]  foreign  [Music]
 

